# Template for scripts/secrets-to-purge.txt
#
# Copy this file, then add the literal values you want redacted from the git
# history — one per line. Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored.
#
#     cp scripts/secrets-to-purge.example scripts/secrets-to-purge.txt
#     sh scripts/purge-secrets-from-history.sh          # dry run
#     sh scripts/purge-secrets-from-history.sh --apply  # rewrite
#
# The copy is git-ignored (see the root .gitignore) because it holds real
# secrets in plaintext, exactly like backend/.env. Delete it when you are done.
#
# Values are matched literally, not as patterns, so paste them exactly as they
# appear in the old source — no quotes, no trailing spaces.
#
# You do NOT need to list a MongoDB password that appears inside a connection
# string: the script already finds and redacts any `mongodb://` or
# `mongodb+srv://` URI that carries inline credentials, with no help. This list
# is for opaque literals it cannot recognise on its own — a JWT signing secret,
# an API key, a standalone password.
#
# Add one value per line below this comment block. Example shape only:
#
# some-standalone-jwt-signing-secret
# sk_live_an_api_key_that_was_committed
